                    <tr>
           	<td valign="top" height="100%"><font face="Trebuchet MS, Tahoma, Verdana, Arial, Helvetica" size="2" color="#FFFFFF">
                      <font face="Trebuchet MS, Tahoma, Verdana, Arial, Helvetica" size="2" color="#FFFFFF"><span class="spnMessageText" id="msg"> I've seen a lot of reviews out there and a lot of false information so I thought I throw in my opinions.  I have been playing the stress test of the WoW beta so my impressions are based on that build.<br /><br /><font color="orange">Good things:</font id="orange"><br /><br />1)  The <font color="beige">sound and music </font id="beige">is top notch.<br />2)  The amount of <font color="beige">quests</font id="beige"> is staggering.  They have hundreds of quests already in the game.  You will at times have trouble finishing all the quests before you're too high level to really benefit from the rewards.  My quest journal has yet to empty out and I spend 90% of my time online questing in one form or another.  The quests are varied, interesting and tie nicely to the various storylines.  When you pass an NPC you immediately know if they have a quest for you, without having to stop to "talk" to them.  This speeds up the process and avoids the tedium.<br />3)  Combat is fluid, fun and fast-paced.  Much like Camelot, the combat moves quickly and generally a death is due to pushing too far or simply not paying attention.  You can challenge yourself and your group if you wish or merrily grind away.  Either approach seems viable and perhaps will depend on how well you can pilot your character.  Solo play seems viable for all characters.  Group play seems like a lot of fun.  Raids also seem viable in the current version of the game.<br />4)  The <font color="beige">world</font id="beige"> is big.  You can distinctly tell when you move from one area to another, but they blend extremely well together.  Unlike previous games where a tundra would open into a lush forest, the zones seem to flow nicely together.   The zones all seem to have a unique tileset and environment though so I've yet to see areas that are repeats.<br />5)  The <font color="beige">travel system </font id="beige">is great.  Moving over land on foot is slow, but not tedious.  Mounts are available to higher level characters.  Static routes are also available, much like DAoC, but they take place on winged creatures and offer incredible rides filled with great views and gorgeous scenary.  Express travel is limited to areas you've previously visited so you can't explore via static routes.  You must explore first and they take advantage of the fast travel once the new areas have been explored.  You gain experience when you explore a new area.  Movement in general is easy with the keyboard.  Changing the direction you're looking is most easily accomplished with the mouse.  This keyboard/mouse combination is nicely implemented and easy to master, although I prefer the mouse-click movement of L2/Shadowbane.<br />6)  <font color="beige">Combat</font id="beige"> is much less tedious than in previous games.  You easily flow from casting to melee and back without extra button pressing.  You cast through interrupts in a way that makes sense and special melee styles incorporate nicely in the general flow of combat.  Movement during combat is also easily managed.<br />7)  <font color="beige">Spells and abilities </font id="beige">are interesting and varied.  You don't lose previous spells when you gain a new more powerful version.  This allows your abilities to be used with great variety in combat.  Often I find myself going back to an old version on occasion because the cast time is quick or I only have enough mana for a lower level spell.  In the end, this makes combat more unique and rewards the player that has a strong grasp of their full range of abilities.<br />8)  <font color="beige">Zoning</font id="beige">, loading and other "immersion busters" is pretty much eliminated.  There are zones into instance dungeons, but otherwise the game doesn't hiccup for anything.  Loading into the game and exiting the game is quick and efficient.<br />9) <font color="beige">Maps</font id="beige"> are amazing.  There is a mini-map that is constantly up and shows points of interest; including towns, quest givers, party members, and all sorts of other crap.  There is also a world map which zooms out to the full world and in to the current "zone".  The total map is always available, but the landmarks and specific areas on the map are only presented and named after they've been discovered by your character.  Its a very nice system that can easily go unnoticed.<br /><br /><font color="orange">Stuff that's average:</font id="orange"><br />1)  The <font color="beige">graphics</font id="beige"> are great in my opinion, but I can see how others would not be excited about them.  They are fluid and extremely well done, but they're not intended to be "realistic".  The game world is one of fantasy and you're expected to be immersed in a world that is nothing like our own.  If you can get past that hurdle, you'll love the variety and color and art of the graphics.  If you want realism, you're not goig to like it.<br />2)  The <font color="beige">chat system </font id="beige">is what we've come to expect from MMORPGs.  Its on par with what else is on the market but really doesn't improve on it.<br />3)  The <font color="beige">emote system </font id="beige">is also pretty similar to what other games offer.  No new ground is broken here, except that when you talk you automatically default into an emote that shows your character speaking.  Its a nice little touch, but I wish there was more along the same lines.<br />4)  I'd say they have more <font color="beige">monster models</font id="beige"> than most games, but the top games like EQ and DAoC probably have more.  Granted, I've only seen a small portion of the zones, but I have to believe this is not an area you'll be excited about.  The monster models they use are well done and each has a nice range of "inactive" motions that they do when idle.  I think this will be an area that's added to greatly when new expansions are created.<br />5)  The total <font color="beige">game stability </font id="beige">is great.  For a beta, its rock solid and far ahead of most games.  I crash less in the beta here than in the final release of Lineage.  I expect a game to run solid and so far WoW delivers.<br />6)  The <font color="beige">class balance</font id="beige"> and high level abilities are probably decent, but I haven't had enough experience with higher levels to know for sure.  I think this is the last thing that will get ironed out and I have confidence, from Blizzard's previous projects, that balance will be achieved.  There are a TON of abilities and varieties on the classes so it will take time.<br /><br /><font color="orange">Stuff that is bad:</font id="orange"><br />Nothing that I've seen yet.  However they're pvp system may fall here if they don't iron it out soon.  We'll have to wait and see what the final verdict is and how it plays out.<br /><br /><font color="green">The Olive Grape</font id="green"><br />Molieren - Treasurer and Prophet<br /><img src="http://www.monstarla.com/images/moli.jpg" border="0"><br /></span id="msg"></font>
